<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- 08-MOBILE-SPEC §8 (F-304): microphone capture for the audio:start
         pipeline. Without this Android 6+ silently denies the runtime request
         and audio:start fires but zero chunks reach the server (F-2063). -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />
    <!-- Android 13+ runtime notification permission, declared for the foreground
         keepalive service specified as F-2335 in docs/rebuild/08-MOBILE-SPEC.md
         §8 ("前台保活服务 … socket 连接期启动 … LOW 通知渠道").
         🔴 MEASURED 2026-08-19: THAT SERVICE DOES NOT EXIST YET, so nothing
         requests this permission. Greppable, so the claim can be re-checked
         rather than believed: the manifest declares no <service> at all; the
         Dart tree's only runtime permission request is the microphone
         (audio_capture.dart, platform_mic_permission.dart); no notification
         plugin is in pubspec.yaml (flutter_foreground_task, which would declare
         this permission itself, is not a dependency — 0 hits in pubspec.lock).
         The previous comment said "wired in a later card", which is a claim
         nobody can grep — anti-façade ④ — and it had been true for long enough
         that no one re-checked it.
         ⚠️ THIS IS A DECISION OWED BEFORE A PLAY SUBMISSION, not a note. The
         store playbook's permission-minimisation row commits us to naming the
         line of code that uses each declared permission; today this one has no
         such line. Either F-2335 lands, or this declaration comes out — the
         same reasoning that removed READ_MEDIA_IMAGES below on the same day.
         It is kept for now because F-2335 is specified, not speculative, and
         removing-then-restoring a line is churn; it is written down here so the
         choice is made deliberately rather than found at submission time. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
    <!-- Install-time normal permission — release builds do NOT inherit it from
         the Flutter debug manifest, and without it every socket/HTTP call dies
         with Permission denied (2026-07-24 real-device release finding). -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <!-- R6 T-4 image pipeline (「+」panel album image).
         Android 13+ (API 33): image_picker uses the system Photo Picker, which
         grants access to the ONE item the user chose and needs no runtime
         permission.
         🔴 In-place correction (2026-08-19): this paragraph used to end with
         "READ_MEDIA_IMAGES is declared so the older gallery intent path and any
         OEM fallback still resolve". That was a justification for a line no
         code path ever exercised — see the ST-4 block further down for what was
         measured and why the declaration is gone. The sentence is corrected
         rather than deleted because it is the reason someone wrote the line.
         Android 12 and below (API ≤32): the document/gallery intent needs
         READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE, capped at maxSdkVersion=32 so the broad legacy
         storage permission is NOT requested on modern devices.
         When the OS refuses either, image_picker raises a PlatformException
         which platform_image_picker.dart turns into a NAMED failure — a picker
         that opens onto nothing is exactly the silent failure the red line
         forbids (same lesson as R6-R5's lock-screen case). -->
    <!-- GA-30 QR-scan pairing (owner 2026-07-26:「扫码优先于手输」— "scan-to-pair
         takes priority over manual entry"). mobile_scanner asks
         for this at runtime the first time the pairing sheet opens; without the
         manifest entry the request is denied outright by the OS and the sheet
         would show a black rectangle. The refusal path is still handled in-app
         (errorBuilder → fall back to manual entry + explain why) — a permission the user declines
         must degrade loudly, not silently.
         `android.hardware.camera` is declared NOT required so the app still
         installs on a camera-less device; manual entry covers that case. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
    <!-- REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES USED TO BE HERE, and where it went is the
         point (ST-1, 2026-08-19). It now lives in the DIRECT flavour only —
         android/app/src/direct/AndroidManifest.xml — because Play forbids a
         store-delivered app from installing an APK to update itself, and a
         manifest line is not behind any build define. Keeping it in main and
         remembering to strip it for the store build is exactly the shape this
         repo does not accept; the flavour source set makes it impossible
         instead. The runtime half is lib/src/update/install_source.dart. -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="false" />
    <!-- ST-4 (2026-08-19): READ_MEDIA_IMAGES USED TO BE DECLARED HERE AND IS GONE.
         Nothing ever requested it. The app reaches photos only through the
         system picker (image_picker -> Android photo picker), which hands back
         a URI and needs no permission at all; the only runtime request in the
         Dart tree is the microphone (audio_capture.dart requests
         Permission.microphone, platform_mic_permission.dart is the gate) — grep
         "Permission." across apps/mobile/lib and there is no photo request to
         find. The plugin does not declare it either: image_picker_android's own
         manifest carries a FileProvider and the backported-picker module
         dependency, no uses-permission line. So this app's line was the only
         source of the declaration.
         MEASURED on a tablet before removing it: with the permission reported
         granted=false by dumpsys, the Photo row still opened the picker and
         listed the gallery.
         A permission that is never requested and is not needed to do the thing
         is not free — it is one more sensitive access to justify in Play's Data
         safety form, and one more line a reviewer reads as a claim about what
         we take.
         READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE below STAYS: it is capped at API 32, the device
         measured above is newer than that, and the pre-33 path was NOT measured
         here. Removing it on a guess is the shape this repo does not accept. -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        android:maxSdkVersion="32" />
    <!-- Card P8 — keep FlowMic's on-device data off Google auto-backup and
         Android device-to-device transfer.

         WHY: the timeline database (flowmic_timeline.db,
         lib/src/timeline/timeline_sqlite.dart) stores transcribed speech in
         PLAINTEXT sqflite plus a lowercased `search_text` copy — nothing in
         it is encrypted at rest. Pairing/session tokens
         (lib/src/auth/token_storage.dart) are live credentials that let a
         restored copy of this app rejoin a paired PC. Leaving the platform
         default (android:allowBackup defaults to true when the attribute is
         absent) means Google's cloud auto-backup or a device-transfer flow
         could carry a user's transcribed speech and live pairing state onto
         another device or into Google's backup storage without the user
         ever being asked "do you want your FlowMic history to go too?".

         DECISION (owner): exclude FlowMic's data from both channels.
         android:allowBackup="false" is the actual, authoritative control —
         it disables cloud auto-backup AND device transfer outright, on
         every API level this app supports. dataExtractionRules (API 31+)
         and fullBackupContent (API 24-30, this app's minSdkVersion) are
         added on top even though allowBackup="false" makes the OS ignore
         them: see res/xml/flowmic_backup_rules.xml for why they are kept
         as documentation + a backstop rather than left out.

         🔴 HONEST TRADEOFF: a user who sets up a new phone via Google
         account restore, or moves to a new device via Android's built-in
         transfer flow, will NOT get their FlowMic conversation history or
         PC pairing carried over — they start with an empty timeline and
         must re-pair every PC from scratch. This is the deliberate cost of
         not letting plaintext transcripts and live pairing tokens leave the
         device through a channel the user did not explicitly choose.

         Deliberately NOT touched: android:usesCleartextTraffic (unrelated —
         that governs plaintext network transport, not on-device backup). -->
    <!-- 🔴 THE NAME UNDER THE ICON IS THE BRAND, and until 2026-08-19 it was
         not: this said `flowmic` (lower case) from the first Flutter skeleton
         commit onwards — a scaffold default nobody ever chose, while README,
         the privacy policy, every in-app string and the store listing all say
         FlowMic. It was never a decision, so it is not being overturned; it is
         being finished. The iOS half is CFBundleDisplayName / CFBundleName in
         ios/Runner/Info.plist, changed in the same commit — a name that differs
         between the two phones is the same defect twice. -->
    <application
        android:label="FlowMic"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/flowmic_backup_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/flowmic_backup_rules_legacy">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!-- owner 2026-07-27 image copy: ClipData.newUri needs a content:// URI, so
             the single clipboard preview file in the private cache is exposed
             through its own FileProvider. Authority is suffixed
             (.clipboard.fileprovider) so it cannot collide with the one
             image_picker declares (.flutter.image_provider).
             🔴 W8-5 (2026-08-10, measured on a real tablet): the sentence above
             is true and it guarded the wrong collision. Both providers used to
             name androidx.core.content.FileProvider directly, and a shared
             android:name collides regardless of how distinct the authorities
             are — see FlowMicFileProviders.kt for the mechanism. Each authority
             now gets its own subclass; the original wording is kept because the
             image_picker part of it still holds.
             exported=false + grantUriPermissions=true is the required pair: the
             system clipboard service grants read access to whichever app pastes,
             and nobody else can reach it. See ImageClipboard.kt. -->
        <provider
            android:name=".ClipboardFileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.clipboard.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/flowmic_clipboard_paths" />
        </provider>
        <!-- Card UP-2b in-app update slice 2: the system package installer runs in
             another process, so the verified .apk has to be handed over as a
             content:// URI. A SECOND provider with its own authority and its own
             paths file — flowmic_clipboard_paths.xml states that widening it
             would expose the rest of the cache to whatever app reads the
             clipboard, so this does not touch it.
             exported=false + grantUriPermissions=true is the required pair: read
             access is granted per-Intent (FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION in
             UpdateInstaller.kt) and nobody else can reach the file.
             🔴 W8-5: android:name must stay a class that no other <provider>
             names. It shipped as androidx.core.content.FileProvider in 0.2.59
             and 0.2.60, which made every self-update on those builds fail at
             the hand-off to the installer. -->
        <provider
            android:name=".UpdateFileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.update.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/flowmic_update_paths" />
        </provider>
        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>

        <!-- 0.2.67 — the privacy-policy / terms links on the data-flow disclosure
             page open in the external browser via url_launcher.

             🔴 THIS BLOCK IS REQUIRED AND THE PLUGIN DOES NOT SUPPLY IT.
             Measured on this machine: url_launcher_android 6.3.17's own
             AndroidManifest.xml declares only a WebViewActivity — no <queries>
             at all. Since Android 11 (API 30) package visibility is opt-in, so
             without this entry `launchUrl` cannot see any browser, returns
             false, and the links fall through to the copy-the-URL path on
             essentially every current device. That failure is LOUD by design
             (data_flow_disclosure_page.dart surfaces it), which is exactly why
             it would have shipped: the feature would look implemented, degrade
             politely, and never work.

             targetSdk comes from the Flutter SDK (see build.gradle's note) and
             is 36 today, so API 30+ behaviour is not hypothetical here. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
            <data android:scheme="https"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
